WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.000
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.476
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:17.980
Unit seventeen: Economic Indicators. Today you will make predictions with the future perfect and future continuous.

4
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.781
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:25.256
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:27.663
Gross domestic product.

7
00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:33.378
Gross domestic product means the total value of goods and services a country produces.

8
00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:36.842
Gross domestic product rose by five percent.

9
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.625
Your turn. Say: Gross domestic product.

10
00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:44.979
Gross domestic product.

11
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:46.915
Inflation rate.

12
00:00:47.917 --> 00:00:50.961
Inflation rate means how fast prices are rising.

13
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.979
The inflation rate fell to two percent.

14
00:00:54.583 --> 00:00:57.526
Your turn. Say: Inflation rate.

15
00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:01.751
Inflation rate.

16
00:01:02.517 --> 00:01:04.238
Consumer price index.

17
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:09.618
Consumer price index means a measure of the average price of everyday goods.

18
00:01:10.217 --> 00:01:13.145
The consumer price index guides wage talks.

19
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:17.714
Your turn. Say: Consumer price index.

20
00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:22.299
Consumer price index.

21
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.481
Unemployment figure.

22
00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:29.201
Unemployment figure means the official number of people without jobs.

23
00:01:29.817 --> 00:01:32.606
The unemployment figure surprised analysts.

24
00:01:33.217 --> 00:01:36.264
Your turn. Say: Unemployment figure.

25
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.833
Unemployment figure.

26
00:01:41.583 --> 00:01:43.072
Interest rate decision.

27
00:01:44.083 --> 00:01:48.567
Interest rate decision means a central bank's choice to change borrowing costs.

28
00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:51.647
The interest rate decision is due on Thursday.

29
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:55.193
Your turn. Say: Interest rate decision.

30
00:01:58.033 --> 00:01:59.545
Interest rate decision.

31
00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:01.981
Economic forecast.

32
00:02:02.983 --> 00:02:07.096
Economic forecast means a prediction about future economic conditions.

33
00:02:07.683 --> 00:02:10.054
The economic forecast is cautious.

34
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.894
Your turn. Say: Economic forecast.

35
00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:18.454
Economic forecast.

36
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:20.554
Leading indicator.

37
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:25.465
Leading indicator means a measure that signals changes before they happen.

38
00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:27.968
New orders are a leading indicator.

39
00:02:28.583 --> 00:02:31.561
Your turn. Say: Leading indicator.

40
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.831
Leading indicator.

41
00:02:36.583 --> 00:02:37.956
Recession signal.

42
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.389
Recession signal means a warning that the economy may soon shrink.

43
00:02:42.983 --> 00:02:45.296
Falling sales were a recession signal.

44
00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:49.035
Your turn. Say: Recession signal.

45
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.493
Recession signal.

46
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.777
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:58.617 --> 00:03:04.601
Visal, a forecast says 'By next year, the economy will have grown by three percent.' What is 'will have grown'?

48
00:03:05.717 --> 00:03:13.280
The future perfect: will have + past participle. It describes an action that will be complete by a future time.

49
00:03:14.383 --> 00:03:15.559
And 'will be growing'?

50
00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:26.875
The future continuous. It describes something in progress at a future time: 'This time next year, the economy will be recovering.'

51
00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:30.285
Which indicators do analysts watch?

52
00:03:31.383 --> 00:03:42.732
Gross domestic product, the inflation rate, the consumer price index and leading indicators. A recession signal makes forecasts more cautious.

53
00:03:43.867 --> 00:03:45.680
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:46.517 --> 00:03:50.815
Grammar focus: Future perfect and future continuous.

55
00:03:51.115 --> 00:03:52.302
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:53.017 --> 00:03:56.212
By next year, the economy will have grown by three percent.

57
00:03:57.117 --> 00:04:00.428
This time next year, the economy will be recovering slowly.

58
00:04:01.317 --> 00:04:04.477
By June, inflation will have fallen below two percent.

59
00:04:05.433 --> 00:04:13.937
Future perfect = will have + past participle (finished by a future time). Future continuous = will be + -ing (in progress at a future time).

60
00:04:14.683 --> 00:04:18.369
Be careful! Don't say: By June, inflation will fall.

61
00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:22.995
Say: By June, inflation will have fallen.

62
00:04:23.295 --> 00:04:26.084
Use the future perfect for completed actions.

63
00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.657
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:31.793
Reading the Numbers.

65
00:04:32.093 --> 00:04:35.822
Economists use indicators to read the health of an economy.

66
00:04:36.833 --> 00:04:42.235
Gross domestic product measures output, while the consumer price index measures the cost of everyday goods.

67
00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:47.729
By next year, the inflation rate will have fallen, if the interest rate decision succeeds.

68
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:54.332
A leading indicator such as new orders may be a recession signal long before unemployment figures change.

69
00:04:55.333 --> 00:04:57.658
No single number tells the whole story.

70
00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:02.757
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.617 --> 00:05:06.626
By next year, the economy blank by three percent.

72
00:05:06.926 --> 00:05:11.323
A, will have grown. B, will grow have. C, has will grown.

73
00:05:14.867 --> 00:05:16.750
The answer is A: will have grown.

74
00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:21.878
A measure that signals change before it happens is a blank indicator.

75
00:05:22.178 --> 00:05:25.437
A, lagging. B, leading. C, central.

76
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.733
The answer is B: leading.

77
00:05:31.883 --> 00:05:35.590
The total value of goods and services a country produces is blank.

78
00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:41.296
A, the price index. B, an unemployment figure. C, gross domestic product.

79
00:05:44.833 --> 00:05:47.831
The answer is C: gross domestic product.

80
00:05:48.983 --> 00:05:50.867
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:59.209
A, This time next year, I will be studying.. B, This time next year, I will have study.. C, This time next year, I studying..

82
00:06:02.767 --> 00:06:06.380
The answer is A: This time next year, I will be studying..

83
00:06:07.517 --> 00:06:10.271
According to the reading, no single number tells…

84
00:06:10.571 --> 00:06:14.642
A, the whole story. B, any story. C, the date.

85
00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.896
The answer is A: the whole story.

86
00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:25.043
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:25.883 --> 00:06:30.251
Which economic indicator do you think matters most to an average family?

88
00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:40.481
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.781 --> 00:07:03.602
I think the inflation rate matters most to an average family, because it shows how fast the cost of food, rent and transport is rising. If wages grow more slowly than prices, a family will have lost purchasing power by the end of the year. Unemployment figures matter as well, but inflation affects everyone every day.

90
00:07:03.902 --> 00:07:05.588
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:06.483 --> 00:07:11.655
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future perfect and future continuous.

92
00:07:11.955 --> 00:07:17.336
Today's value is economic literacy. Understand how money and work shape people's lives.

93
00:07:17.636 --> 00:07:20.416
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:20.716 --> 00:07:23.879
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

